method.description:
A [NetwAction] for a one-off act such as firing or placing something, bound to the method [param authority] the server runs to judge it.
[param authority] is a method on an entity root or one of its children. Repeated calls for the same method return the same [NetwAction].
[codeblock]
@onready var place_bomb := Netw.action(_place_bomb)

func _ready() -> void:
    place_bomb.predict = _show_bomb_now

func _place_bomb(ctx: NetwActionContext) -> void:
    var bomb := BOMB.instantiate()
    ctx.bind(bomb)
    bombs.add_child(bomb)
[/codeblock]

Declares what the server does with each player it lets in, and returns the [NetwJoinConfig] that can [method NetwJoinConfig.quantize] the extra values they sent.
[codeblock]
func _init() -> void:
    Netw.configure_join(self, spawn_at)

func spawn_at(who: NetwParticipant, point: StringName, team: int) -> NetwSceneHandle:
    arena.add_child(Netw.spawn_player(who, _spawn_avatar, team))
    return Netw.scene(arena)
[/codeblock]
[param handler] takes the [NetwParticipant] first and the values passed to [method join] after it. The participant is seated before the handler runs, so it is already the player's own handle, and [member NetwParticipant.join] is the [ResolvedJoin] frame behind it. What it returns is where the player ends up. A [NetwSceneHandle], or a [Node] inside one, puts the player in that world and lets them in as one act, which fires [signal NetwSceneHandle.participant_entered]. Nothing means [param handler] placed the player itself. A [NetwPromise] is waited for and then read as one of those two, which is how a player is loaded from a save before being placed. Anything else turns the join down and reports [signal NetwConnectHandle.join_failed].
Declare it from [method Object._init] on a node in the session's branch. A [param handler] taking extra values is a named method rather than a lambda, because its parameter types are how both sides agree on what those values are. A session declaring nothing uses [NetwDefaultJoin]. Two live declarations on one session turn every join down rather than placing players somewhere the game never chose. Pass [code]Callable()[/code] to clear the declaration.

Registers [param callable] so [method rpc] and [method request] may call it, and returns the [NetwMemberConfig] that declares what [code]@rpc[/code] cannot.
[codeblock]
func _init() -> void:
    Netw.configure_rpc(self.aim).controller().quantize(
            NetwQuantizeAngle.new().bits(12).centered(),
    )

@rpc("any_peer", "unreliable")
func aim(yaw: float) -> void:
    turret.rotation.y = yaw
[/codeblock]
The declaration belongs to the script rather than to the node, so every instance declares the same thing from [method Object._init]. A method on a child of the entity root also becomes a component through [method NetwEntity.register_component], which is what lets a call name it with one byte rather than a path.

Registers [param callable] as a function [method spawn] may run on every peer to build the same node.
[codeblock]
func _init() -> void:
    Netw.configure_spawn(_spawn_bullet)

func _spawn_bullet(dir: Vector2, tier: int) -> Node:
    var bullet := BulletScene.instantiate()
    bullet.setup(dir, tier)
    return bullet
[/codeblock]
It lives on a node that exists on every peer, and it builds the new node from its arguments alone, since those arguments are all the other peers receive. What a field was holding travels separately, through [method NetwPropertyConfig.on_spawn], and is applied once the function has run.

The [NetwConnectHandle] of the session [param node] is in, which is where the servers this peer can reach are listed and where its [MultiplayerPeer] is built.
[codeblock]
var c := Netw.connection(self)
var ticket := c.create_peer(
        ENetMultiplayerPeer,
        NetwMultiplayer.TRANSPORT_MODE_HOST,
        "", {port = 21253}, _created)

func _created(peer: MultiplayerPeer, error: Error, detail: String):
    if error == OK:
        Netw.of(self).multiplayer_peer = peer
[/codeblock]
The callback runs once, in a later frame. Assign the peer inside it or let it go, since [method NetwConnectHandle.create_peer] hands it over exactly once.

What [param entity] held at [param tick], read back as a copy that the game may keep.
[codeblock]
func _place_bomb(ctx: NetwActionContext, pos: Vector2) -> void:
    var past := Netw.sample(entity, ctx.view_tick)
    if not past.has_value(&"position"):
        ctx.deny()
        return
[/codeblock]
Nothing recorded at [param tick] returns an empty record rather than [code]null[/code], so a caller asks [method NetwRecord.has_value] rather than checking for a missing object. Writing to what comes back changes nothing that was recorded.
